    "md5": "f584bd340ff353166c67c6e837bfe688",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1987,
    "chunk_text": "Surplus that starves\n\nPresident and Finance Minister Anura Kumara Dissanayake and his Government\u2019s real test was never stabilisation\n\nTwo years in, the NPP Government has mastered collecting money: Spending it is another matter\n\nIn September 2024, Sri Lanka handed the National People\u2019s Power the cleanest mandate in its history. Two years on, the question is no longer whether the Government means well. It is whether it can deliver.\n\nFirst, remember what was handed over. The NPP did not inherit a collapse; it inherited a recovery. In 2022, the economy had shrunk 7.3% and the country had defaulted on its foreign debt. By September 2024, under the IMF programme agreed in March 2023, growth was back to 5% in the first half of the year. Inflation stood at \u22120.5%, reserves had rebuilt to about $ 6 billion, and interest rates had been cut by 7.5 percentage points. Two days before the election, the outgoing Government reached an agreement in principle with international bondholders. The hardest part of the rescue was done. The test for the new Government was never stabilisation. It was what came next.\n\nThe new Government has built on that inheritance, and the record deserves credit. Growth held at 5% in 2025. Per capita income reached an all-time high of $ 5,003. Inflation stayed below zero for most of 2025. The stock market doubled from about 11,300 points at the election to 22,624 by December 2025. Tax revenue rose from 12.3% to 15.4% of GDP in a single year, and the primary surplus hit a record 5.4% of GDP. Government debt fell from 98.4% to 94.9% of GDP. These are real achievements, and any honest scorecard begins with them.\n\nYear one was a good year for stability: low inflation, falling rates, record revenue and a booming market. Year two has reversed most of those gains, and the oil shock explains only part of it.\n\nA budget surplus is earned in two ways: by raising more or by spending less. This Government has done both, and the second is the problem."
