    "chunk_text": "The signals there are mixed at best. Foreign direct investment reached $ 1.06 billion in 2025, a genuine improvement. But in the first half of 2026 it slowed to about $ 450 million, against a full-year target of $ 1.5 billion. Exports did better: $ 17.25 billion of goods and services in 2025, and up 8% in the first half of 2026. Tourism tells a harder story. Arrivals hit a record 2.36 million in 2025, but earnings rose only 1.6%, so each visitor spent about 12% less. In 2026, arrivals are slightly down on last year.\n\nNorth-East Asia stayed calm, and not by luck. Japan held about 254 days of oil reserves and released 80 million barrels in March. South Korea held about 208 days and capped pump prices for the first time in almost 30 years. China held about 120 days and Taiwan more than 100. They built their buffers in good years, for exactly this kind of bad one.\n\nSri Lanka had about six weeks of fuel. Within days, motorists were rationed to 15 litres a week, and from 16 March State offices, schools and universities moved to a four-day week. Sri Lanka has never built a strategic reserve, and that failure belongs to every Government, not this one alone. But it marks the difference between managing a crisis and preparing for one.\n\nPreparing means scenario planning. Every year, a Government should ask what happens to fuel supply, the rupee and the Budget if oil reaches $ 100 or $ 130, or if the Gulf closes, and have the reserves, supply contracts and contingency funds ready before it does. Singapore has run a Government scenario-planning unit since 1991. Sri Lanka still plans for the year it hopes for, not the year it may get."
