    "chunk_text": "Preparing means scenario planning. Every year, a Government should ask what happens to fuel supply, the rupee and the Budget if oil reaches $ 100 or $ 130, or if the Gulf closes, and have the reserves, supply contracts and contingency funds ready before it does. Singapore has run a Government scenario-planning unit since 1991. Sri Lanka still plans for the year it hopes for, not the year it may get.\n\nFor borrowers, the turn has been sharp. Lending rates rose from 11.56% to 12.16%, and longer-dated Government Bonds now yield 12 to 12.5%. For a household with a pawning loan, or a small firm on an overdraft, that is the cost of living by another name. The rupee lost 7.2% in the first five months of the year and briefly touched Rs. 348 in the interbank market in May, before the Central Bank stepped in. A weaker rupee raises the price of every litre of fuel, every bag of fertiliser and every box of medicine the country imports.\n\nSome of this is imported, and no Sri Lankan Government controls the price of oil. But a shock tests the buffers a Government has built. Reserves stand at $ 6.85 billion, barely above the $ 6.8 billion of December 2025, and short of the $ 8 billion promised to the IMF for year-end.\n\nTo its credit, the Central Bank has not printed money to fund the Government. Monetary discipline has held. Fiscal execution has not.\n\nIn June, the IMF cut its 2026 growth forecast for Sri Lanka to 3%, down from 4% before the Middle East war. The Government\u2019s own target had been 5 to 6%.\n\nSlower growth lands hardest on the young. An IMF comparison published in September found that 43% of Sri Lanka\u2019s young people with post-secondary education are unemployed. That is the highest of 12 Asia-Pacific economies. National figures point the same way. Unemployment rose to 4% in the second quarter of 2026, and among 25- to 29-year-olds it jumped from 9.3% to 12.1% in a year. Net, the economy added only about 74,000 jobs over the year, while