    "chunk_text": "Slower growth lands hardest on the young. An IMF comparison published in September found that 43% of Sri Lanka\u2019s young people with post-secondary education are unemployed. That is the highest of 12 Asia-Pacific economies. National figures point the same way. Unemployment rose to 4% in the second quarter of 2026, and among 25- to 29-year-olds it jumped from 9.3% to 12.1% in a year. Net, the economy added only about 74,000 jobs over the year, while agriculture shed 174,000.\n\nFamilies are borrowing to get by. Pawning advances rose from Rs. 660 billion in December 2024 to Rs. 1,012 billion by March 2026, up 53% in fifteen months. Credit card balances rose 17% over the same period, to Rs. 197 billion. Higher gold prices explain part of the pawning surge, but not why so many households are putting their jewellery on the counter. With State investment stalled and the 2027 capital budget cut, the jobs that would lift these families out are not being created.\n\nOn the surface, households are spending again. Private consumption grew 9.1% in 2025 and 7.1% in real terms in the first quarter of 2026. But incomes have not caught up. At the end of 2025, real wages in the public sector were still 16.1% below their 2020 level, and in the informal private sector 11.8% below. The gap is being filled with credit. Private sector lending was growing 27% a year by April 2026, on top of the rise in pawning and card debt. Retailers now report shoppers buying only essentials, with inflation above 7%.\n\nAn economy is sustained by what its people save and invest, not by what they borrow to consume. Here the trend runs the wrong way. In its December 2025 review, the IMF projected national savings falling from 28.7% of GDP in 2024 to 23.2% in 2026, and national investment from 27.0% to 23.6%. The State saves by not spending its investment budget, while households borrow to stand still. That is not a recipe for growth."
