    "chunk_text": "Before the election, Anura Kumara Dissanayake promised to cut electricity bills by a third. Tariffs fell 20% in January 2025, then rose 15% in June 2025, well before any war. The Ceylon Electricity Board ended 2025 with a Rs. 38.7 billion loss. In February 2026, still before the Gulf erupted, it asked for a 13.56% increase, and from April tariffs rose by 8% to 14%. The war came on top, opening a Rs. 38 billion hole for mid-2026, which the regulator kept off 95% of consumers with a Rs. 15 billion subsidy. Low-quality coal, a procurement failure, cut output at Norochcholai. Most of the electricity problem was made at home. A third off was the promise, and voters remember it.\n\nIt was not an isolated failure. Around $ 625,000 meant for the United States Postal Service went astray through a phishing scheme, most of it in 2025. Two departments, the same weakness: payment systems that no one was watching.\n\nPublic safety has not improved. Police recorded 114 shootings and 60 deaths in 2025, more incidents than the 103 of 2024. By mid-September 2026, underworld-linked shootings had killed 17 people, including two children in a grenade attack in Dehiwala. The police service is short of about 40,000 officers.\n\nCyclone Ditwah, in November 2025, killed more than 600 people. The World Bank put direct damage at $ 4.1 billion, about 4% of GDP. Parliament approved over Rs. 500 billion for recovery projects. Nearly a year on, the opposition is still asking for a report on how much has been spent. The IMF\u2019s latest mission called for removing the bottlenecks in capital spending to speed up reconstruction."
