    "chunk_text": "Cyclone Ditwah, in November 2025, killed more than 600 people. The World Bank put direct damage at $ 4.1 billion, about 4% of GDP. Parliament approved over Rs. 500 billion for recovery projects. Nearly a year on, the opposition is still asking for a report on how much has been spent. The IMF\u2019s latest mission called for removing the bottlenecks in capital spending to speed up reconstruction.\n\nThe digital agenda deserves recognition. GovPay, the platform for paying Government charges online, has processed more than Rs. 5 billion across over 300 State institutions. Service charges on Government e-payments were scrapped in January 2026. The first digital identity cards are due by the end of this year. These are the foundations of a modern State, and they were built quietly and well.\n\nThe NPP inherited a country on the road to recovery and kept it there through 2025. That counts. But a surplus built on unspent hospital and road budgets is not prudence. It is postponed development. When the first real shock arrived in 2026, the cushion proved thin.\n\nIf 2027 is to look better, the President must run the Government by delivery. Every key Ministry, from health and education to agriculture, energy and highways, should have three or four published targets for the year. Spending and progress against them should be reported in public, every month. Malaysia did this through PEMANDU, its delivery unit, from 2009; Britain did it through the Prime Minister\u2019s Delivery Unit. Ministers who deliver should be backed. Those who do not should be moved, whatever their standing in the party.\n\nThat means spending the capital budget on time and in full. It means fixing the payment controls that let millions walk out of the Treasury, giving the families rebuilding after Ditwah an account of where the money went, and putting jobs for the young at the centre of the 2027 Budget."
