    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Experienced graduates,  yes \u2014 but never at the cost of rigour\n\nA reply to Nuwan Jayawardena\u2019s guest column \u201cThe expert shortage we are building\u201d (1 October 2026)\n\nWhen Nuwan Jayawardena asked me, as an academic, what I made of his views in \u201cThe expert shortage we are building\u201d, published on 1 October (- https://www.ft.lk/columns/The-expert-shortage-we-are-building/4-798011), I did not need long to answer. I agree with him. He writes as a CFA charterholder and a practitioner who hires; I write after twenty years in the lecture hall, including my years as Head of the Department of Finance at USJ. We have arrived at the same place through different doors.\n\nHis central sentence deserves to be pinned on the noticeboard of every faculty in the country: universities must stop producing internship-ready graduates and start producing experienced ones. Yes. Firms that once spent years shaping graduates are no longer willing to, and AI is hastening that retreat. The cost of building judgment has been handed back to us. We can accept it or watch the senior pipeline he describes run dry.\n\nBut let me say plainly where my own conviction lies, because anyone who has sat in my classroom knows I have one. Academic programs are at a crossroads, and the way forward is not lighter theory but stronger theory, taught differently. The graduate the market now needs is more technical than ever and, at the same time, more firmly grounded than ever. Nuwan says it himself: a graduate without foundations does not become AI-leveraged. Access to knowledge is cheap. Understanding is not.\n\nNo curriculum succeeds if it is designed for the student we used to have rather than the one sitting in front of us. Today\u2019s Gen Z undergraduates are not prepared to learn passively, and I do not blame them. They have grown up with a device in hand, and long, uninterrupted concentration does not come easily. A two-hour lecture delivered to them is, quite simply, lost on many of them."
