    "chunk_text": "No curriculum succeeds if it is designed for the student we used to have rather than the one sitting in front of us. Today\u2019s Gen Z undergraduates are not prepared to learn passively, and I do not blame them. They have grown up with a device in hand, and long, uninterrupted concentration does not come easily. A two-hour lecture delivered to them is, quite simply, lost on many of them.\n\nThat is precisely why Nuwan\u2019s prescription persuades me. Real problems, mentors and defending one\u2019s work under questioning are not only what employers want. They are exactly what this generation needs to find its footing.\n\nAs an academic in Finance my proposal rests on three pillars, each developed across all four years:\n\nPillar 1 \u2014 Know Finance: financial knowledge and conceptual foundations.\n\nPillar 2 \u2014 Analyse, Build and Validate: financial analytics, technology and problem-solving.\n\nPillar 3 \u2014 Demonstrate Professional Judgment: industry practice, decision-making and professional accountability.\n\nBloom\u2019s Revised Taxonomy \u2014 remember, understand, apply, analyse, evaluate, create \u2014 is the ladder that connects them. I am firm on one design choice: we do not assign one pillar to one rung, with theory at the bottom and practice at the top. That is the old model, where the university teaches and the employer finishes the job. All three pillars climb together, so judgment is practiced from the first year rather than discovered in the last.\n\nAt the top of the first pillar, a student critically assesses competing theories and brings them to bear on a problem she has never seen. At the top of the second, she designs an analytical solution, tests its assumptions and challenges AI outputs rather than accepting them. At the top of the third, she delivers an original solution to a real finance problem and defends it before people qualified to question her. That is the defensible judgment Nuwan rightly calls the most valuable capability of all."
