    "chunk_text": "At the top of the first pillar, a student critically assesses competing theories and brings them to bear on a problem she has never seen. At the top of the second, she designs an analytical solution, tests its assumptions and challenges AI outputs rather than accepting them. At the top of the third, she delivers an original solution to a real finance problem and defends it before people qualified to question her. That is the defensible judgment Nuwan rightly calls the most valuable capability of all.\n\nTake a valuation project on a real listed company. Students apply what they know about financial statements, cash flows, discount rates and risk. They build a discounted cash flow model, run sensitivity analysis, compare it with market evidence and test any AI-generated assumptions. Then they recommend a valuation range, explain its uncertainty to an industry panel and defend it, individually, under questioning.\n\nI do not mark them on whether the number is \u201cright\u201d. I mark them on whether they can explain it, test it and stand behind it. This is what Nuwan calls examining the person, not the artefact. When the artefact can be produced in seconds, it is the only assessment that still tells me what a graduate can really do.\n\nHere I must be candid, as my students would expect of me. Nuwan proposes that 60 to 70 per cent of the final two years be spent on paid, real work. I share the aim, and his point that a paid student is properly supervised is well made. My caution concerns proportion, and I offer it for all of us to think about."
