    "chunk_size_bytes": 1796,
    "chunk_text": "Here I must be candid, as my students would expect of me. Nuwan proposes that 60 to 70 per cent of the final two years be spent on paid, real work. I share the aim, and his point that a paid student is properly supervised is well made. My caution concerns proportion, and I offer it for all of us to think about.\n\nThe three pillars give Nuwan\u2019s ideas a natural home. His discovery phase fits first-year observation of industry. His problem briefs become the material of the second and third years. His paid, supervised work and defended outputs crown the fourth. I also share his reading of the quieter obstacles: industry supervision must count towards promotion as research does, placement brokerage should be professionally staffed, and practice-track appointments would add capacity without stretching existing staff.\n\nWhere will the next expert come from? From the universities, as Nuwan says, but only if we change what a degree is. Not a timetable of content followed by the hope that industry will finish the job, but a deliberate journey in which students learn finance, use technology to test it, and learn to make and defend professional decisions, with a guiding hand beside them all the way.\n\nTo firms: bring us your problems, data and panels from the first year, not only placement slots in the last. To professional bodies: help us standardise what counts. To fellow academics: the three pillars are not an extra burden; they describe what good teaching was always meant to achieve. And to our students: we will guide you, but we will not lower the bar.\n\nThe teaching hospital, as Nuwan reminds us, has always known that judgment cannot be learned from a book. It has also always known that judgment cannot be built without one. Our degrees must now stand on both truths at once."
