    "chunk_size_bytes": 1523,
    "chunk_text": "Real relief is engineered, not announced\n\nEvery month, salaried Sri Lankans see tax leave their payslip before they see their pay. For them, the debate on income tax relief is a question of whether the household budget balances after food, transport, school fees and loan instalments. The Government has indicated a proposal to raise the monthly tax-free threshold from Rs. 150,000 to Rs. 200,000 as revenue strengthens, with the timing and design still under discussion, and its stated medium-term policy is to lower tax rates while broadening the base and improving compliance. That intent is understandable and welcome. The real question is how relief is delivered.\n\nThe personal relief was raised from Rs. 1.2 million to Rs. 1.8 million from April 2025, and the first taxable band was widened from Rs. 500,000 to Rs. 1 million, with the 12% band removed. Some observers have reasonably cautioned that a further increase should be weighed against its revenue cost and the narrowness of the base. Those concerns deserve to be taken seriously, and they sharpen the case for careful design.\n\nThe base is narrow. Of the 2,547,322 employees in the Inland Revenue Department\u2019s employer returns for 2024/25, about 75.6% had no tax deducted, which is consistent with earnings below the taxable threshold. On my earlier estimate, drawing on IRD and Labour Force Survey data, the system reliably reaches only around a quarter of the employed population. For 2024/25, only 25% of individual income tax returns were filed on time."
