    "chunk_text": "The balance between corporate and non-corporate taxpayers is the other side of the same question. The IRD\u2019s 2025 data show that corporate taxpayers contributed Rs. 669.6 billion, or 59% of income tax collections, up from 57% in 2024. Non-corporate taxpayers contributed Rs. 461.7 billion, or 41%, down from 43%. Corporate collections grew by about 15% over the year, against about 4.5% for the non-corporate group. The corporate figure includes corporate income tax, dividend tax and remittance tax, while the non-corporate figure includes individual and partnership income tax, APIT , AIT and withholding taxes. The split is therefore by type of tax, not strictly by type of taxpayer.\n\nConsider, purely as an illustration and not as any official proposal, reinstating an intermediate 12% slab on the next Rs. 500,000, with the personal relief of Rs. 1.8 million unchanged. The rates would then run: 6% on the first Rs. 1 million, 12%, 18%, 24% and 30% on each successive Rs. 500,000, and 36% thereafter. The 36% rate would begin at Rs. 400,000 a month rather than Rs. 358,333. The effect on three illustrative earners is shown below.\n\nThe relief is largest in proportion for the Rs. 300,000 earner, who pays about a fifth less tax. For higher earners the benefit is capped at Rs. 10,000 a month, or Rs. 120,000 a year, so the cost per taxpayer is bounded. The total cost depends on how many taxpayers fall in each band, which the available IRD data, based on the earlier 2024/25 structure, cannot show. The Ministry of Finance and the IRD are best placed to compute and publish that figure preferably before the Budget. Importantly, nobody is removed from the net and everyone above Rs. 150,000 a month remains a taxpayer.\n\nThis is an illustration of design, not a finished proposal. The final width and rates are for policymakers, who must weigh the revenue effect and the available fiscal space. The principles, however, are clear:"
