    "md5": "7e3b1c9988043b0046cdae8a5b8bba25",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1924,
    "chunk_text": "Are Sri Lanka\u2019s corporates ready  to meet rising shareholder activism?\n\nIn 2023, cloud software giant Salesforce became the target of a historic, multi-front campaign led by a swarm of high-profile activist investors, including Elliott Investment Management, ValueAct Capital, Starboard Value, Third Point, and Inclusive Capital. They cited Salesforce\u2019s slowing revenue growth, bloated cost structure, and history of expensive, dilutive acquisitions like Slack and Tableau as the raison d\u2019\u00eatre for their actions. They demanded an immediate pivot from aggressive expansion to operational efficiency, margin expansion, and disciplined capital allocation.\n\nFaced with escalating pressure and the threat of costly public proxy contests, CEO Marc Benioff and Salesforce\u2019s leadership responded with swift corporate transformations. Salesforce initiated sweeping workforce reductions, cutting 10% of its staff and downsizing its real estate footprint to dramatically curb operating expenses. To demonstrate stronger corporate governance, the company refreshed its Board of directors, appointed ValueAct CEO Mason Morfit to the Board, and disbanded its dedicated strategic M&A committee to restrict further buyout sprees. Furthermore, Salesforce launched aggressive share buyback programs and eventually paid out its first-ever quarterly dividend, funneling billions of dollars directly back to shareholders. The collaborative restructuring paid off rapidly as Salesforce reported surging operating margins and robust free cash flow, driving a sharp rally in its share price. Satisfied with the swift operational pivot and improved financial performance, firms like Elliott withdrew their proposed Board slates. For me, this event stands as a textbook example of how concentrated activist pressure can rapidly force a tech giant to transition from a growth-at-all-cost mindset into a high-margin, disciplined profit engine."
  },
