    "chunk_size_bytes": 1104,
    "chunk_text": "To survive and thrive in this climate, corporate Boards must stop being reactive. Modern executives must perform rigorous \u201cactivist\u201d audits on their own operational portfolios, proactively engage in open, ongoing dialogue with top shareholders, and articulate clear, long-term value-creation narratives.\n\nActivism in Sri Lanka currently centres on minority protections during mergers, capital restructurings, and transparency issues. Recently, minority shareholders of Associated Motor Finance PLC (AMF) pushed for minority safeguards during the company\u2019s mandatory amalgamation into L B Finance PLC. After the company offered Rs. 55 per share to buy out minority investors and delist from the Colombo Stock Exchange, dissenting shareholders rejected the cash consideration as inadequate. Exercising statutory rights under Section 93 of the Companies Act No. 7 of 2007, minority investors formally demanded that the Board purchase their shares at a fair and reasonable value. Despite these objections, LB Finance finalised the merger, resulting in AMF\u2019s share cancellation and license revocation."
