    "chunk_size_bytes": 1897,
    "chunk_text": "During this bi-annual exercise, the Board must direct its focus toward five critical areas tailored to the Sri Lankan corporate landscape. The primary area of scrutiny is capital allocation. Activists frequently target companies holding excessive cash reserves or underutilised real estate earning sub-par yields. Boards must proactively justify these retained earnings against clear hurdle rates or return capital to shareholders through dividends and share buybacks. A second critical focus is the conglomerate discount that often impacts Sri Lankan diversified entities. Complex crossholdings and non-core business segments can obscure underlying asset value, making it necessary for directors to conduct regular sum-of-the-parts valuation analyses and evaluate potential divestment or unbundling strategies.\n\nTo survive and thrive in this climate, corporate boards must stop being reactive. Modern executives must perform rigorous \u201cactivist\u201d audits on their own operational portfolios, proactively engage in open, ongoing dialogue with top shareholders, and articulate clear, long-term value-creation narratives\n\nTo translate these focus areas into concrete outcomes, each bi-annual meeting must generate specific action items. The Board should mandate benchmarking of total shareholder return and price-to-book performance against domestic and regional peers over multi-year horizons. Independent perception audits must be conducted prior to these meetings to catch early signs of shareholder discontent. Additionally, directors should periodically conduct brief tabletop simulation exercises to assess response readiness across legal, regulatory, and public relations channels. By rigorously examining these core areas twice a year, Sri Lankan Boards can effectively eliminate the vulnerabilities that attract activist attention while building enduring trust with their shareholder base."
  },
