    "chunk_size_bytes": 1900,
    "chunk_text": "The RSS supplies were particularly limited. Just 2,844 kilogrammes were offered across the month\u2019s four auctions, with no offerings at the final two sales. Grades RSS2 to RSS5 remained unquoted throughout October, while RSS1 was quoted only at the first two auctions.\n\n\u201cWith the domestic raw material availability remaining tight, the manufacturers increasingly relied on imported lower-grade RSS to meet their production requirements,\u201d the report said.\n\nPremium latex crepe 1X averaged Rs.1,187.50 per kilogramme in October, compared with Rs.1,000 a year earlier, an increase of 18.8 percent. No.1 averaged Rs.1,186 per kilogramme, up 19.8 percent from Rs.990.\n\nFurther down the grade range, No.2 averaged Rs.1,098.75 per kilogramme, up 30 percent YoY, while No.3 rose 42.7 percent to Rs.1,067.50. No.4 recorded a sharper 70.2 percent increase to Rs.1,059.50 per kilogramme.\n\nThe annual movements substantially narrowed the price gap between the premium and lower latex crepe grades. The difference between 1X and No.4 averaged Rs.128 per kilogramme in October, compared with Rs.377.50 a year earlier.\n\nOn the report\u2019s monthly comparison, No.3 and No.4 prices increased by Rs.60.10 and Rs.61.30 per kilogramme, respectively, against a Rs.2.50 rise for premium 1X.\n\nThe scrap crepe prices also generally strengthened during the month, although the movements varied by grade. The average for 1X Brown increased by Rs.52.50 to Rs.997.50 per kilogramme, while 2X Brown and 4X Brown gained Rs.14 and Rs.4.41, respectively. In contrast, 3X Brown edged down Rs.1.83.\n\nThe broker attributed the overall firmness in the scrap crepe to sustained buying interest and limited supplies.\n\nThe latex crepe accounted for 207,100 kilogrammes of October auction offerings, followed by skim rubber at 47,375 kilogrammes and scrap crepe at 34,550 kilogrammes. RSS represented less than one percent of the total."
