    "md5": "a05f77988fb71ffc539267c993877ab8",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1983,
    "chunk_text": "Digital payment histories pitched as new collateral for SMEs\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s digital payment ecosystem exceeds 2.2mn transactions daily, with around Rs.82bn average value\n\nDigital transaction-generated data called \u201cliquid gold\u201d that generative AI can turn into personalised, trusted services\n\nSL targets to increase digital economy\u2019s share of GDP from 3-4% to over 12% by 2030\n\nThe banks were urged to look beyond today\u2019s creditworthy clientele and lend to small merchants on the strength of their digital payment records, as the policymakers addressed the CEOs Night that opened the inaugural Sri Lanka FinTech Festival 2026 in Colombo yesterday.\n\nOrganised by the FinTech Forum Sri Lanka in collaboration with the Digital Economy Ministry, the invitation-only evening at Shangri-La Colombo drew the bank and fintech chief executives, regulators and government officials, with former DBS Bank Chief Innovation Officer Neal Cross delivering the keynote address.\n\nDigital Economy Deputy Minister Eranga Weeraratne framed the challenge around a Kurunegala shopkeeper accepting her first digital payment, arguing that the real test was whether she would offer the option to her next customer.\n\n\u201cWe can encourage her first transaction but we have to earn the second,\u201d he told the gathering.\n\nWeeraratne noted that, over time, the shopkeeper\u2019s digital payment record could unlock formal credit for her business.\n\n\u201cFor an SME with limited physical assets to attract lending, her financial history can be the new collateral,\u201d he remarked, while urging the banks to partner with fintech firms and help build the customers who could qualify for credit tomorrow rather than only those who qualify today.\n\nThe government, he outlined, would back the drive through promotion programmes such as the gamified Lucky Draw and possibly by subsidising the transaction costs, along with a Fund of Funds and regulatory frameworks for qualified venture capital funds and\u00a0virtual assets."
