    "chunk_text": "The government, he outlined, would back the drive through promotion programmes such as the gamified Lucky Draw and possibly by subsidising the transaction costs, along with a Fund of Funds and regulatory frameworks for qualified venture capital funds and\u00a0virtual assets.\n\nDr. Amarasekara pointed to LankaQR, JustPay, CEFTS and GovPay as the rails on which future innovation would travel, adding that the CBSL was using regulatory sandboxes to help promising ideas reach the market.\n\nHe also observed that high-frequency payment data was giving the central banks richer insights into spending, credit conditions and the transmission of monetary policy.\n\n\u201cPublic trust takes years to build and moments to lose,\u201d he cautioned.\n\nDescribing the banks and fintechs as complementary rather than competing, Dr. Amarasekara asserted, \u201cA financial system cannot be innovative if it is unstable. Neither can it be stable if it fails to innovate.\u201d\n\nHowever, Chief Advisor to President on Digital Economy and GovTech Sri Lanka Chairman Dr. Hans Wijayasuriya argued that innovation must solve the real problems rather than merely produce clever services if the digital economy\u2019s share of GDP is to rise from 3-4 percent to over 12 percent by 2030, which would require that share to grow three to fourfold.\n\nHe urged the industry to close gaps in inclusion, trust and language and to connect the top of the economic pyramid with the bottom, so that the largest businesses transact with the smallest.\n\nHe also described the data generated by every digital transaction as \u201cliquid gold\u201d that generative AI could turn into personalised and trusted services."
