    "chunk_text": "Custodial deaths are inimical to national interests\n\nTwo custodial deaths\u00a0 have rocked the National People\u2019s Power (NPP) government, amidst allegations by opposition parties and local and human rights activists that the government is giving increased powers to the police to detain individuals for anextremely long duration through new laws.\n\nIn one incident, 29-year-old Ishara Harsha Kumara, a resident of Kiriwaththuduwa, died under suspicious circumstances while in the custody of the Homagama Police Station on October 25, 2026.\n\nThe medical report on the suspect who died while in Homagama Police custody has revealed that he had three broken ribs and bleeding\n\nWestern Province Senior DIG Sajeewa Medawatte said investigations have revealed that the young suspect was assaulted during a clash between two groups on October 24, a day before his arrest. The suspect had claimed he was hospitalised after falling off a bicycle. However, police claim that investigations revealed he had been assaulted and later left hospital without medical approval.\n\nWhat is the relevance of these claims about falling from a bicycle and assault during a clash? Do the police attempt to attribute the death of the youth to those incidents? However, the very action by the police clearly negates those attempts. It is common knowledge that hardly a man would be able to walk into a police station or at least to his home with three broken ribs and\n\nPolice admitted the man to Homagama hospital on the day after he was arrested. It also negates the validity of the claims about\u00a0 falling from the bicycle and assaulted during a clash."
