    "chunk_text": "Although assaulting the suspects - leave alone torturing - in police custody is illegal, it is common knowledge that it is a norm in police stations. Conversely. even the most innocent law breaker would not voluntarily reveal or accept his culpability for an offence, unless some sort of pressure is exerted on him. Suspects in high-profile cases\u00a0 reportedly refuse to reveal the passwords of their computers which are believed to contain information crucial to national security. Needless to say, this a tricky issue for police officers.\n\nMorality apart, human rights in individual countries are closely linked to the economic cooperation within the international community. Issues related to GSP+ and labour rights are cases in point. This is highly important for a country like Sri Lanka, which is recovering from a brutal economic crisis through formidable sacrifices made by the people, especially the downtrodden.\n\n\u2018Your Thought\u2019 is a space, a right of the readers to support or contradict and discuss the issues highlighted in the editorial and other articles in the editorial and op-ed pages. Designed as the reader\u2019s editorial; our readers can send in their writings, with a word count not exceeding 200, to \u2018Your Thought\u2019, Daily Mirror Political Features Desk, No 8, Hunupitiya Cross Road, Colombo 2 or email to [email\u00a0protected]"
