    "md5": "59a10610726a68b7d97561f5f3df7125",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1902,
    "chunk_text": "Ex-Presidency: A Promise We Keep Postponing\n\nYes, I am ready to stand as a single-issue presidential candidate. I will serve for six months only, within which time I will abolish the Executive Presidency and institute a parliamentary system by constitutional methods. The roots of bribery, corruption, nepotism and bad governance lie in the Executive Presidency.\u201d\n\nThose words, spoken by the Rev. Maduluwave Sobitha Thero in 2014, captured a conviction that had been growing for years and has never been properly settled: that placing so much authority in a single office is the central flaw in Sri Lanka\u2019s system of government.\n\nSobitha Thero\u2019s tireless efforts to resolve the constitutional crisis, abolish the Executive Presidency and secure a lasting devolution settlement\u00a0 made him the favourite of many\u00a0 for the role of \u201cCommon Candidate\u201d.\u00a0 (Photo courtesy of Artworks)\n\nThe 1978 Constitution was a decisive departure from the past. The Westminster model, in which the Head of State and the Head of Government are different people, a President and a Prime Minister, gave way to an arrangement that gathered executive power into one pair of hands. J. R. Jayewardene, its chief architect, had nurtured the idea for a long time. He regarded rising crime, theft, burglary, alcoholism, illicit liquor and sexual violence as grave problems obstructing development. Only a strong and stable administration under an Executive President, he believed, could meet the demands of an open economy.\n\nHe had made the case in Parliament as early as 1966, as Minister of State. \u201cA strong executive seated in power for a specific period, not subject to the whims and fancies of an elected legislature will not be afraid of taking correct but unpopular decisions,\u201d he said, according to Hansard. \u201cThis seems to me a very necessary requirement in a developing country faced with grave problems.\u201d"
