    "md5": "995f345aef61475af88d1f932be5b420",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1742,
    "chunk_text": "He had made the case in Parliament as early as 1966, as Minister of State. \u201cA strong executive seated in power for a specific period, not subject to the whims and fancies of an elected legislature will not be afraid of taking correct but unpopular decisions,\u201d he said, according to Hansard. \u201cThis seems to me a very necessary requirement in a developing country faced with grave problems.\u201d\n\nDr. Perera set out his objections at length in his book \u201cA Critical Analysis of the New Constitution of the Sri Lankan Government\u201d, published soon after the new charter took effect. The fifth chapter examines the character of the presidency itself. The eight chapters that follow explain how the office could come to dominate Parliament, the Prime Minister and Cabinet, the judiciary and the electoral process. Many of the abuses he described had not yet occurred in his lifetime, yet his analysis now\u00a0reads like a forecast.\n\nPerera was no casual critic. He held two doctorates, a PhD and a DSc, for work on comparative constitutional reform and parliamentary procedure, based on his research with European Constitutions. He argued that a constitution in a diverse society needs checks and balances and must seek the widest possible common ground. He foresaw a clash between a Parliament leaning left of centre and an Executive leaning right, especially where the President appoints the Prime Minister from among its members. He also pointed to countries that had adopted presidential systems and then suffered coups, political turmoil and economic hardship. Sri Lanka, he cautioned, should not gamble on an untested model by discarding fifty years of parliamentary democracy that, for all its flaws, had delivered stability and progress."
