    "chunk_text": "Perera was no casual critic. He held two doctorates, a PhD and a DSc, for work on comparative constitutional reform and parliamentary procedure, based on his research with European Constitutions. He argued that a constitution in a diverse society needs checks and balances and must seek the widest possible common ground. He foresaw a clash between a Parliament leaning left of centre and an Executive leaning right, especially where the President appoints the Prime Minister from among its members. He also pointed to countries that had adopted presidential systems and then suffered coups, political turmoil and economic hardship. Sri Lanka, he cautioned, should not gamble on an untested model by discarding fifty years of parliamentary democracy that, for all its flaws, had delivered stability and progress.\n\nThe question returned with force in the months before the 2015 presidential election. Debate over abolition gathered momentum as the deadline approached, driven by two groups. The first was determined to end Rajapaksa rule. The second, including a few within the governing alliance, sincerely favoured abolishing the post but expected Mahinda Rajapaksa to carry on from within the legislature.\n\nOpposition parties, civil society organisations and professional bodies scrambled for a candidate, rather like prize-hunters at an Avurudu festival. Two obstacles stood in the way. One was finding someone trustworthy enough to relinquish power after winning an office of near-limitless authority. The other was finding a figure who could match the incumbent\u2019s popularity and charisma. Sobitha Thero\u2019s tireless efforts to resolve the constitutional crisis, abolish the Executive Presidency and secure a lasting devolution settlement had made him the favourite of many in opposition ranks for the role of \u201cCommon Candidate\u201d."
