    "chunk_text": "The Palestinian city of Gaza has been reduced to rubble. According to the World Health Organisation (WHO) and the UN Office for the Co-ordination of Humanitarian Affairs (OCHA), 34 out of 36 hospitals (94%) in the Gaza Strip have been damaged. Roughly half\u00a0 the health facilities are either partially functioning or completely out of service.\n\nUNICEF reported that nearly 97% to 98% of school buildings and all universities in the Gaza Strip have been damaged or destroyed since October 2023.\n\nMany believed that democracy in Israel was under attack, and they particularly opposed the effort by Netanyahu\u2019s government to weaken the power and independence of the Israeli judiciary, which sparked massive street protests in 2023.\n\nThey also resented Netanyahu\u2019s government allowing ultra-Orthodox men to avoid military service, despite the Israeli military\u2019s growing manpower shortage. On a personal level, Netanyahu is widely perceived as being dishonest. He is also still on trial for corruption.\n\nIt is in this context that the October elections were held. For the colonised people of Palestine,\u00a0 there is little difference regarding whichever Israeli party wins the election \u2013they are both dedicated to the ethnic cleansing of Palestine and continued genocide of Palestinians.\u00a0 That election itself has now come to an end,\u00a0 and no party has received sufficient numbers to form a government.\n\nBenjamin Netanyahu, the outgoing Prime Minister,\u00a0 and his extreme right-wing colleagues in the out-going regime have won 53 seats in the Knesset. The opposition faction, also a coalition of parties led by Gadi Eisenkot\u2019s \u2018Yashar Party\u2019, won a total of 48 seats. The Palestinian-Israeli Parties (two Arab-majority lists, the Joint List and Ra\u2019am) who form around 20% or more of Israel\u2019s population won 19 seats.\n\nYet, to form a government, one or the other Israeli parties need at the least 61 seats in the Knesset to form a government\u2026 and therein lies the problem for both Netanyahu and Eisenkot."
