    "md5": "b0c4c38827ee53fa9c4f51b2725b5558",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1901,
    "chunk_text": "National programme planned to ensure children\u2019s online safety\n\nThe Government has begun preliminary discussions to establish an inter-ministerial national programme to ensure children\u2019s online safety and digital well-being.\n\nThe initial discussion was held at Temple Trees on November 2 under the leadership of Deputy Minister of Digital Economy Eranga Weeraratne.\n\nThe meeting focused on identifying the responsibilities and future roles of key ministries in developing a coordinated national framework to protect children from online abuse, cyberbullying and other digital threats.\n\nAddressing the meeting, Secretary to the Prime Minister Pradeep Saputantri said children faced various challenges amid the rapid development of digital technology.\n\nHe stressed the need to move beyond isolated interventions and establish a national programme bringing together State institutions, experts and international organisations.\n\nHe said the proposed programme would focus on empowerment, safety, accountability-based design, protection, response and governance.\n\nDeputy Minister Eranga Weeraratne said the Government was taking steps to provide internet access to all secondary schools by the end of this year and extend the facility to primary schools next year.\n\nHowever, he highlighted that providing children with access to technology must go hand in hand with ensuring a safe digital environment.\n\nHe also said Sri Lanka hoped to provide policy leadership on children's online safety at regional and international levels by engaging with other South Asian countries.\n\nRepresentatives from the World Health Organization (WHO) and UNICEF made technical presentations at the meeting, while Community Health Specialist Dr. Asiri Hewamalage shared information based on international research and evidence.\n\nAnanda Galappatti also presented a proposed digital framework covering the entire State sector."
