    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "e3e2517b415050f5e7e15f558a004699",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1822,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018Please allow us to tell our stories\u2019:  Theepachelvan on the struggles of Tamil writers\n\nPoems and novels are powerful in two different dimensions. Poetry serves as a voice against oppression and as a medium that instantly records the pain of the oppressed. Only a novel can comprehensively record the deep roots of politics, the multifaceted contradictions of society, and the scars of history across time and space\n\nThe Human Rights Commission of Sri Lanka (HRCSL) issued a landmark ruling recently on the release of two books by the prominent Tamil writer B. Pradeepan\u2014also known as Theepachelvan\u2014which had been detained by the Customs for months. The commission stated that the detention of the books was a direct violation of the Tamil author\u2019s Fundamental Rights. In the following conversation, Theepachelvan, a novelist and poet living in Kilinochchi, reflects on the grievances of Tamil-speaking writers against the backdrop of what he calls State Oppression and the marginalization of ethnic minorities.\n\nQThe Human Rights Commission (HRC) recently recommended releasing your books detained by the Customs, yet they are still being held. Furthermore, another Tamil writer recently faced the same issue when copies of his novel were detained by Customs in Jaffna. How do you respond to these recent developments?\n\nThis is not just a problem of a single writer, nor is it the first time this issue has happened. It is part of a systematic suppression directed against Tamil books and Tamil writers. Whenever a book is written by Tamil writers like us, the State machinery approaches it with the prejudice that it should be against the State and the armed forces.\n\nIn this context, even though the Human Rights Commission recommended the immediate release of my books, the government has not released them."
  },
