    "description": "UK to double wait for permanent residency, with exemptions for care workers",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/international/UK-to-double-wait-for-permanent-residency-with-exemptions-for-care-workers/107-354057",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793690882.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-03-dailymirrorlk-897a088d-0000",
    "chunk_index": 0,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "9bedfcbacc5b40fe907cb783ad34ef81",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1696,
    "chunk_text": "UK to double wait for permanent residency, with exemptions for care workers\n\nALJAZEERA - The United Kingdom has revised its immigration rules, doubling the time most foreign workers must wait to qualify for permanent residency to 10 years and making refugees who arrive on \u201csmall boats\u201d wait as long as 30 years.\n\nHome Secretary Shabana Mahmood, speaking in Parliament on Monday, said hundreds of thousands of care workers will be exempt from the hardline rules, offering a concession amid a political outcry within the governing Labour Party.\n\nPermanent residency, known in the UK as indefinite leave to remain, carries the right to work, study, use the free National Health Service and claim welfare benefits. Until now, it has been granted after five years.\n\nThe new rules, set to take effect next year, tie eligibility for permanent residency to income.\n\nPeople who earn more than 50,000 pounds a year ($67,000) will still be able to apply after five years, and those earning more than 125,000 pounds ($167,500) after three. Everyone else will be placed on the 10-year path. The median salary in the UK is about 39,000 pounds (roughly $52,000).\n\nThe rules will apply retroactively to workers already in the country.\n\n\u201cSettlement should not be automatic. It should be earned,\u201d Mahmood, the home secretary, told Parliament, adding that the changes would restore fairness by linking the right to settle to what immigrants contribute.\n\n\u201cThose who contribute most will settle fastest,\u201d she said. \u201cThose who don\u2019t play by the rules will be refused or wait longer.\u201d\n\nThe UK government first proposed last year that lower-skilled workers wait 10 to 15 years for permanent residency."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-11-03-dailymirrorlk-897a088d",
    "num": "dailymirrorlk-897a088d",
    "date_str": "2026-11-03",
    "description": "UK to double wait for permanent residency, with exemptions for care workers",
    "url_metadata": "https://www.dailymirror.lk/international/UK-to-double-wait-for-permanent-residency-with-exemptions-for-care-workers/107-354057",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "dailymirrorlk",
    "time_ut": 1793690882.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-11-03-dailymirrorlk-897a088d-0001",
    "chunk_index": 1,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "6df2b9dfc2de2a53891dbfb650c541ba",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1666,
    "chunk_text": "\u201cThose who contribute most will settle fastest,\u201d she said. \u201cThose who don\u2019t play by the rules will be refused or wait longer.\u201d\n\nThe UK government first proposed last year that lower-skilled workers wait 10 to 15 years for permanent residency.\n\nUnder the revised plan, care workers will be able to settle after five years if they have met the conditions of their visas and earn at least 25,000 pounds ($33,500) a year.\n\nMahmood said the rules would offer a path for care workers \u201cwho worked hard and answered our call when we needed them\u201d.\n\nAs for refugees, those who arrive through one of the government\u2019s \u201cnew safe and legal routes\u201d will be able to settle after five years, she said. For other refugees, the standard wait will become 20 years, reduced to 10 years for those who work or study. Anyone who arrived by an \u201cillegal method\u201d, such as by small boat, could wait up to 30 years for settlement, she said.\n\nUnder the new rules, applicants will also be disqualified if they have a criminal record, cannot speak English \u201cto a high standard\u201d, owe money to the government, or have not made at least three years of national insurance contributions.\n\nMahmood said the overhaul would mean that half a million people would settle later than they otherwise would have, saving the government billions of pounds in benefits.\n\nIn total, the government estimates that as many as 1.4 million people will receive settled status between 2026 and 2030.\n\nThe changes, announced after a 12-week public consultation that drew more than 200,000 responses, are part of a broader effort to tighten immigration rules after arrivals surged following Brexit."
  },
