    "chunk_size_bytes": 2010,
    "chunk_text": "Trump has put himself center stage by crisscrossing the country to deliver a succession of speeches for Republican candidate, despite opinion polls showing his approval ratings falling to record lows of between 33 and 37%.\n\nDemocrats have sought to reinforce the message that the election is about Trump while trying to highlight the perceived corruption of the US president and his family, along with affordability issues and the impact of the conflict with Iran, which is seen as having exacerbated the cost of living.\n\nOpinion surveys have shown that voters have become increasingly concerned about corruption in government. A recent Harris poll for the Guardian found that 76% think elected officials care more about enriching themselves than improving the economy for ordinary people.\n\nDisclosures to the US office of government ethics show that Trump has made about $2.2bn since returning to the White House, including royalties from his own name-branded meme coin.\n\nAs the president has grown wealthier, voters have chafed under rising living costs, which Trump vowed to reduce during his 2024 presidential campaign.\n\nThe war with Iran \u2013 which Trump launched in February in tandem with Israel \u2013 has stoked inflation by increasing global oil prices, which has led to higher prices at the pump for American motorists and raised the cost of diesel, a vital fuel for supply chains and farmers.\n\nThe US elections also take place under a shadow of uncertainty about whether Trump will accept the results if they do not go the Republicans\u2019 way.\n\nAlaska is just one of the cases being pursued by the justice department \u2013 under the control of Trump\u2019s close ally, Todd Blanche, the attorney general \u2013 that opponents say are designed to cast doubt on the election\u2019s integrity, including through baseless claims of mass voting by non-US citizens.\n\nThe department plans to deploy a record 1,000 election monitors to polling stations across the US. Critics have denounced the move as voter intimidation."
